{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1792873020222,"sessionID":"ses_e908f9a9affe7nZ9nxUMdJwwnf","part":{"id":"prt_16f8f15ba001zBUd2lxz1ocfBE","sessionID":"ses_e908f9a9affe7nZ9nxUMdJwwnf","messageID":"msg_16f8f0930001T564EI8KrnJfY8","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I've grasped the full context of §4. Update with the client's answer: April pró-labore (pro-labore) was paid on April 30, 2026 (within the period). New primary:\n\n- Pró-labore cash within the period: April portion for comp 05/25–02/26 = 32,000 + 3,200 (April accrual, paid 30/04) = 35,200\n- Folha (payroll) = 193,000 + 35,200 + 56,400 = 284,600\n- Fator R = 284,600/1,020,000 = 27.90% < 28% → Anexo V ✓\n\nUpdate the scenario table:\n- Pessimistic: encargos (social security contributions) for comp 05/25–03/26 only (51,700) + pró-labore 35,200 + salaries 193,000 = 279,900 → 27.44% → V\n- Primary: 284,600 → 27.90% → V\n- Reversal (virada) scenario: only if a March 2026 pró-labore (hidden in the 2026-03 row = 0) actually existed and was paid within the period: 287,800 → 28.22% → III. However, the resumo (summary) line for March has pró-labore 0 — under the new client statement, the \"em aberto (outstanding)\" note refers to the April pró-labore, which is on the 2026-04 row (3,200). So the zero on the March row now has no note and is a true zero accrual. Remaining uncertainty: the note is physically attached to the 2026-03 row — an ambiguity in document placement; also the client's answer contradicts the email (06/05 vs. 30/04), and the comprovante (proof of payment) is pending.\n\nWait, the previous line for \"Pró-labore março\" in §4 said \"(linha 2026-03 = 0, obs. 'em aberto na data do resumo')\". Under the client's answer, the note refers to April's pró-labore; the March row is zero. So update the bullet.\n\nContradiction note: email says 06/05 (\"paguei atrasado, dia 6 de maio, se isso importar\"); the client's latest answer says 30/04, with a PIX posted to the system in May, and a comprovante to be sent later. Use the latest answer as the main axis; if the comprovante shows 06/05, folha reverts to 281,400 → 27.59% → still Anexo V! Good news: both dates → Anexo V. The only factor that could flip it is a possible March pró-labore payment within the period (+3,200 → 287,800 → 28.22%), but no evidence of a March accrual exists (resumo = 0), and the client provided no additional information.\n\nSo actually the result is now very robust: under all documented date readings → Anexo V. Only the hypothetical existence of a March pró-labore not present in the resumo would flip it — and the resumo shows 0 accrual, so there's nothing to pay. Flag it as a residual open item (why March accrual = 0; whether a March/April cash payment existed for an unrecorded accrual) — with a note on materiality: if it exists, the flip.\n\nHmm wait: under the cash rule, any pró-labore paid within the period counts regardless of accrual. If the partners skipped March accrual but, say, paid double in April... No evidence. The resumo shows exactly one anomaly (the 2026-03 zero + note), which is now explained by the April payment/posting story. Under the client's story: April accrual (2026-04 row, 3,200) was paid on 30/04; why is March accrual 0? Unexplained but documented; no cash evidence contradicts it (the May extrato shows only the May pró-labore payment).\n\nHmm, another reading worth keeping in the sensitivity analysis: if the 2026-03 row's zero + note means accounting *shifted* March's pró-labore (3,200) to April — i.e., March accrual was paid on 30/04, and April accrual (3,200 on the 2026-04 row) was paid 06/05 per the email — folha is still = 32,000 (May–Feb) + 3,200 (one payment within the period, regardless of which accrual) = 35,200. Same number. Good — under any single-payment reading it's robust at 284,600; only a double payment within the period (both March and April accruals paid by 30/04, contradicting the 2026-04 row... no, that would require an extra 3,200 somewhere with no evidence) would